# A re-entrant activation (book §12, the S/I2 trace; Codex's reading of the author's norm, accepted 2026-09-28,
# OPUS-CODEX-20260928-10): its occurrence already active on this thread, it keeps its declared fields' working
# values to itself and publishes nothing.  (The row used to pin the fresh instance a re-entry and a call by
# reference ran over -- lmx_fresh, removed by R2: the author, 2026-09-28, no per-call instance in the graph.)
# - `down` calls itself: every inner call is re-entrant.  r = down(3) is 3 + 10 * down(2) = 123 -- each activation
#   reads back its own x and y after the inner calls.  down\x is the outermost activation's 3, published at its
#   first call, and down\y its 12, published at its exit: the inner ones published nothing.
# - `apply` calls `seven` by reference (a callable formal): no activation of seven is active, so it runs over its
#   own occurrence and publishes as any call does -- d = 7, seven\s = 7.
# The exit is 7 when all hold.
fn: down (int: n) int
    int: x n
    int: y 0
    if: n > 0
        y: down(n - 1)
    return: x + 10 * y
end: down

fn: seven () int
    int: s 7
    return: s
end: seven

fn: apply (seven: g) int
    return: g + 0
end: apply

int: r down(3)
int: d apply(seven)
int: dx 0
dx: down\x
int: dy 0
dy: down\y
int: ss 0
ss: seven\s
int: ok 0
if: r = 123 && dx = 3 && dy = 12 && d = 7 && ss = 7
    ok: 7
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
